insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c2cf2ba2-64df-565a-8834-7b1373555e9f', 'rg8-word-parts-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-word-parts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Mural on Fourth Avenue$q$, $q$The wall behind the laundromat on Fourth Avenue had been blank and gray for as long as Lucia could remember. Then, in June, the community arts center chose it for a new mural, and Lucia was one of six teenagers picked to help paint it.

The lead artist, Mr. Tanaka, showed the group his plan. Rather than a single picture, the mural would show the whole spectrum of colors, starting with deep red at one end and fading through orange, yellow, green and blue to violet at the other. Inside each band of color, he wanted scenes from the neighborhood's history.

Lucia's job was the green section. She studied old photographs at the library, looking for details that would be visible from across the street. She chose the corner market, the old trolley tracks and a row of trees that had stood there for decades.

Painting was slower than she expected. Some days the heat made the paint dry too fast, and she had to repaint whole patches. Still, she kept a journal and tried to describe each day's progress in a few sentences.

When the mural was finished, Mr. Tanaka carved an inscription into a stone plaque at the bottom edge: the names of all six student artists and the year. At the unveiling, a woman Lucia had never met asked her for an autograph. Lucia laughed, but she signed the woman's program carefully, in her neatest handwriting.$q$, 'draft')
