-- Passage rg8-word-parts-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b719150c-a79f-551e-8685-73bfe4a7b64e', 'rg8-word-parts-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-word-parts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Listening to the Ground$q$, $q$Most earthquakes are too small for people to feel. Every day, however, instruments around the world record many tiny movements deep underground. Geologists use these records to understand what is happening below the surface.

An earthquake begins when stress builds up along a fault, a crack where two huge sections of rock meet. When the stress becomes too great, the rock suddenly ruptures, and the two sections slip past each other. Waves of energy spread out from that point in every direction.

A seismograph is a device that records those waves. Older models used a pen that moved across a rolling sheet of paper, drawing a jagged line whenever the ground shook. Modern versions store the information digitally, but scientists still think of the result as a picture of the shaking.

Researchers often arrange the readings in chronological order. Seeing the events from first to last helps them notice patterns, such as a series of small shakes that come before a larger one. Even so, no scientist can yet say exactly when a major earthquake will occur.

Some sensors are sensitive enough to detect microscopic shifts in rock, movements far smaller than the width of a hair. Linked together across a region, these sensors give scientists a detailed view of the ground they stand on.$q$, 'draft')
