-- Passage rg8-word-parts-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8847c7c5-aef2-59f7-93b5-2d357de8bddb', 'rg8-word-parts-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-word-parts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Compost Experiment$q$, $q$When the science teacher announced a class contest to cut down on cafeteria waste, Darius and his friend Ingrid signed up right away. Their idea was simple: collect fruit peels and vegetable scraps from lunch and turn them into compost for the school garden.

The first step was sorting. Not everything in the trash could go into the compost bin. Plastic forks and wrappers were not biodegradable, so they would sit in the soil for years without breaking down. Banana peels, apple cores and lettuce leaves, on the other hand, would rot into dark, rich soil within a few months.

Ingrid kept careful notes. She recorded how much the scraps weighed each day and wrote down the temperature inside the bin. Darius was in charge of the hydration of the pile, because compost needs to stay damp, a little like a wrung-out sponge.

Halfway through the month, the bin's thermometer began to malfunction. It showed the same reading no matter what, even after a rainy weekend. Their teacher, Mr. Oyelaran, lent them a new one and joked that even tools need a day off.

At first, Darius was sure the pile would smell terrible. Ingrid contradicted him, explaining that a balanced pile with enough dry leaves should smell like a forest floor. She turned out to be right.

By spring, the garden beds were full of their compost, and the tomato plants were taller than anyone had expected.$q$, 'draft')
