-- Passage rg8-word-parts-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e4c35e27-2411-507c-936d-39049ee24786', 'rg8-word-parts-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-word-parts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Water on the Move$q$, $q$More than two thousand years ago, engineers in ancient Rome faced a problem. Their city was growing quickly, and the nearby river could not supply enough clean water for everyone. Their solution was the aqueduct, a long channel that carried water from distant springs into the city.

Building an aqueduct required careful planning. The channel had to slope downward the entire way, because the system relied on gravity rather than pumps. If the slope was too steep, the rushing water would wear away the stone. If it was too gentle, the water would barely move.

Much of the work was manual. Laborers cut stone and hauled it into place, then sealed the joints with a type of cement. For long stretches, the channels ran beneath the hills, and these subterranean sections kept the water cool and protected it from dirt.

Where the route crossed a valley, builders raised the channel on tall stone arches. Some of these arches still stand today, and visitors from around the world come to see them.

Inside the city, the water was conducted through smaller pipes to public fountains, baths and some private homes. Officials kept track of who received water, and people who secretly tapped into the pipes could be fined.

Modern cities still move water over long distances. Engineers today use pumps and steel pipes, but the basic idea of guiding water from one place to another would be familiar to the builders of Rome.$q$, 'draft')
